.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_FREE_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_free_entry \- free an entry back to the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool_free_entry(mp, entry);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns an allocated entry to the free list of the pool and sets the
caller's pointer to NULL.

The caller names the pool; the macro does not derive it from the entry.
Free an entry to the pool that it came from: passing a different pool is
an error of the caller, and it is fatal.

The pool decides from the address alone whether the entry is one of its
own, before it reads anything through it. An entry inside the buffer of
the pool is checked in the status array of the pool. The pool also
records the address of every entry that its dynamic fallback hands out,
until that entry is freed, and checks any other address against that
record. An entry of another pool, an address that the pool never handed
out, and a fallback entry that is already free all end in the assert,
and none of them is read.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp     Pool that the entry came from
entry  Entry to free. The macro sets it to NULL afterward
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
It is safe to call this macro with a NULL entry. A NULL pool is an error
of the caller, and it is fatal
The macro does corruption detection with assertions

On a thread-safe pool the entry goes into a small cache of the calling
thread, and the macro reaches the shared free list only when that cache
is full. A pool too small to keep a cache (fewer than 8 elements) takes
the lock of the pool on every call instead, and so does a thread that
the pool has no cache left to give: one lock for each call, as on a pool
without a cache. See ccol_mempool_create(3) for how to size a pool.

The cache does not change corruption detection or double-free detection:
an entry in a cache is still marked free, so a second free of it is
detected exactly as it is otherwise. The one exception is two threads
that free the same entry at the same instant. The check that catches a
double free runs outside the lock, so one of the two threads can miss
the mark of the other. Such a call is a data race in the caller's
program in either case, and it is the same limit that a general purpose
allocator with a thread cache has.

The macro evaluates each argument exactly once. entry must be a
modifiable lvalue, such as a variable or an element of an array, so an
expression with a side effect, such as entries[i++], frees and clears
one element.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers do not contend on the common path, and the pool can
hand an entry that one thread freed out to another thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_alloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_free_entry (3)
